  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 351 PM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nRain showers are on the doorstep of central Indiana as a low \npressure system makes its way into the region. The low is expected \nto slowly track eastward through the weekend, providing shower and \nsome embedded thunderstorms from this afternoon and throughout the \nweekend. \n\nWhile CAMs are not greatly reflective of the ongoing precipitation, \nat least in terms of timing and location, it does continue to show \nthat this initial round of rain is likely to diminish as it moves \ninto the forecast area. As the atmosphere continues to moisten from \nthe southerly flow, additional rounds of rain will continue to form \nover the area late this evening to overnight and continuing into the \nday Sunday. Saturday afternoon looks to have the best chance of \nflash flooding concerns with PWATs near 2\" and isolated convection \ndumping heavy rain at times so take precautions if out driving. For \ntotal weekend rain totals, widespread amounts of 1-2, maybe up to 3 \ninches is expected but locally higher amounts are also probable. \nWhere the locally higher amounts end up is the question and will \ndepend on the speed at which the low progresses, the exact track it \ntakes, and any mesoscale forcing that is struggling to be resolved \nin models. Highest totals will typically be found in the comma head \nof the low, which at this time looks to most likely be in the NW \nportion of the forecast area and areas north of there. \n\nIn addition to flooding, there is an isolated chance of severe \nweather for Saturday afternoon and potentially into the evening \nhours. Damaging winds is the main threat but can't completely rule \nout some rotation happening, either in the form of cold air funnels \nor maybe an isolated tornado due to the slow moving surface low \noverhead. \n\nGoing into the new week, more uncertainty arises as the global \nmodels disagree on how far to the east the low and the upper \ntroughing system will reach thanks largely to a Bermuda high in \nplace over the Atlantic. There are times when the low could regress \nback towards central Indiana bringing additional chances for rain \nnext week. Eventually it is expected to get absorbed into the larger \ntroughing pattern to the north and push through New England. Given \nthese unknowns, low confidence in PoPs for next week as well as \ntemperatures. Keeping with guidance which has at least slight to \nchance PoPs each day, particularly during the afternoon hours and \nhighs generally in the 80s.",
